<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludovico Ariosto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1474">1474</a>–<a href="/years/1533">1533</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ludovico Ariosto was an Italian poet at the court of the Este in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>. His romantic epic Orlando Furioso, first published in 1516 and expanded until 1532, continued Boiardo's tale of Orlando and became one of the most influential poems of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. He also wrote comedies and satires and served the Este as governor of the Garfagnana.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludovico-ariosto.jpg" alt="Ludovico Ariosto" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ludovico_Ariosto_1532.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
